  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1240 AM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nCentral mountains and Pikes Peak region could see another round of \nshowers with a brief mix to snow down to 7500 feet Thursday morning \nas wrap around moisture and strong northerly winds around the back \nside of the system spread across southeast CO. Winds could gust up \nto 50 mph across the plains.  Any additional rain/snow accumulation \nThursday morning will be spotty and light and confined to the \nsoutheast mountains according to latest model runs. As winds \ndiminish during the evening and skies clear out, this will set \nthe stage for another frost/freeze event across northern \nportions of the southeast plains. Probabilities look greatest \nacross El Paso and Kiowa counties (40-80%), though there could \nbe some more patchy frost or brief light freezing temperatures \nas far south as the highway 50 corridor and along the southern \nI-25 corridor in Huerfano and western Las Animas County. Will \nhoist a Freeze Watch for El Paso and Kiowa counties where \nprobabilities of freezing temperatures are 50% or greater. Lows \nin the mid to upper 20s could bring an end to the growing season\nacross northern El Paso County. As details become more clear, \nsuspect we may need some Frost Advisories to the south. \n\nTemperatures slowly recover for Friday and odds for a more \nwidespread freeze looks higher for Friday night into Saturday \nmorning for all of the southeast plains as clear skies and dry \nsurface dew points allow for efficient radiational cooling. Lee \ntroughing may keep temperatures slightly warmer along the lower \neastern slopes and I-25 corridor though, so will need to see how\nthese details play out, but suspect another round of \nfrost/freeze highlights will be necessary east of I-25.\n\nOtherwise warmer temperatures return though these will be closer to \nnormal rather than the 10-15 degree normal readings we've had of \nlate.  A weak system in southwest flow will bring a low end chance \nfor afternoon showers and thunderstorms across the mountains on \nSunday.  Then we will be watching the approach of our next upper \ntrough/closed along the west coast which could affect CO sometime \nmid to latter part of next week.  -KT",
